this robot wants to put a stamp on a  package but when it gets there it's too  small  never fear it has a trick up its servos  it takes itself apart and reassembles  into a new shape now it can reach this  is called a modular robot it's key skill  adapting it isn't a first modular robot  but unlike many of its counterparts this  one is able to sense its environment  analyze the challenges it faces and  choose a new configuration to solve them  all on its own  clever lot it's made up of individual  units which researchers call s'mores  they connect each other  magnetically the s'mores units all work  together to achieve tasks and they also  carry around their own brain this tall  black module full of sensors and  processes here the robots been tasked  with picking up a pink object and  putting it in a drop-off zone data from  its camera gradually builds up a digital  map of where it is as it drives and  clocks its targets it locates the pink  object but it won't fit in the narrow  gap so it accesses a library of  different behaviors and configurations  in its memory to find a solution in this  case it reconfigures into a new shape  called the proboscis now it can reach  the object and pull it up but it needs  to take it to the drop-off zone and that  proboscis is long and awkward so it  reconfigures back into the car formation  to complete its task  the robot can also climb stairs in the  snake formation or zip around flat  ground in the Scorpion formation okay  perhaps zip is a bit much right now the  robots pretty slow and sometimes it  needs a bit of help but the researchers  say that this system lays the groundwork  for future BOTS which could reorganize  themselves to suit a whole host of  surroundings robots which they hope one  day could help in search and rescue  missions  what adaptable little machines
 

